The question is about when defaults are provided to the library — at construction time vs. after the fact.

**Construction time only:**
```lua
local db = LibDB:New({
    defaults = { frame = { width = 1100 } }
})
```
All defaults must be known upfront. Simple, but means the library must be created after all defaults are defined.

**RegisterDefaults after construction:**
```lua
local db = LibDB:New({ savedVariable = "MyDB" })
-- Later, possibly from different files:


db:RegisterDefaults("frame", { width = 1100, height = 38 })
db:RegisterDefaults("health", { font = { size = 20 } })
```

This approach lets different modules register their own defaults at any point, which is useful when settings are scattered across multiple files. AceDB uses this pattern with deep-merging. The tradeoff is that cached default values could become stale if defaults change after initialization, and it adds complexity to the implementation.

For RPGBossBar, all defaults live in one file loaded upfront, so construction-time registration works fine. But for a reusable library, other addons might have more modular structures where this flexibility matters.
